 "result": "- Returning `True` from the `except` block reports failed orders as successful, so callers cannot detect a declined charge or a failed insert.\n- `charge()` runs before `db.insert()` with no compensation. If the insert fails, the customer has been charged but no order record exists, and there is no refund or void.\n- `charge()` is called without an idempotency key (such as the order ID), so caller retries or concurrent duplicate submissions can charge the customer more than once.\n- `except Exception` treats payment declines, network timeouts, and DB errors the same way. An ambiguous charge result, such as a timeout after the provider processed it, is never reconciled.\n- `log.warning(\"... %s\", e)` drops the stack trace and logs a failed payment or order at warning level. Use `log.exception`/error level and include the order and customer identifiers so failures can be found and reconciled.",
